  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1017 PM CST Wed Nov 18 2026\n\nThe synoptic pattern aloft has gotten a tad more complex with the \nupper-level low over the Pacific Northwest splitting. One portion \nbeing a small negatively tilted trough over Montana. The other \nportion is a strong and deepening low pressure system over northern \nCalifornia. Much of the rest of the pattern is subtle ridging over \nthe east-central portion of the CONUS. A cold front off the the \neastern low pressure has stalled out extending just to the \nnorth/northwest of the CWA. Winds in that vicinity has dropped to \nlight and variable, with winds in the rest of the area remaining out \nof the south at 5-10 mph. \n\nWith Thursday and Friday looking similar to Wednesday with highs in \nthe 60s and a strong south wind, the most notable weather in the \nnear term is the potential for fog development Thursday morning. \nEnsembles has finally caught up to the HRRR and now shows a strong \nsignal of fog development tonight. Nearly the entire area is \nforecast to have 100% relative humidities tonight. The hinge of the \nextent both spatially and in density appears contingent on how weak \nthe winds can die down. If winds decrease down to 5 mph, around the \ncurrent forecast, widespread fog is expected especially from Scott \nCity to Hays towards the Oklahoma border. If the fog develops as \ndescribed, a Dense Fog Advisory will likely be warranted with \nvisibilities down to 1/4 miles especially in fog prone areas. \n\nThe next notable aspect of the forecast period is the second \nsystem's arrival on Saturday. A strong cold front will limit highs \nfrom getting into the 60s except the far southeastern counties. \nPrecipitation chances has shifted westward considerably as the \nsystem aloft has become better resolved. Now an axis of >15% chances \nof stretches from Hays to Dodge City and eastward. Chances top out \nat around 50% near the eastern counties against central Kansas. \nThunder chances via ensembles remain below 10% with the bulk of the \nthunderstorm chances towards central Kansas. \n\nBeyond Saturday, highs the next few days are in the low to mid 40s. \nTuesday, the next possible system with precipitation. This has \nshifted from rain chances to now chances with temperatures right on \nthe edge. Even if the snow falls, the ground/surface temperatures are \nforecast right around 32 degrees. Take these chances with a grain of \nsalt as a lot may still change being almost a week away.",
